virglrenderer: a memory budget for guest resources

What: every resource a guest makes (3D and 2D resources, blob pipe resources) is
charged its estimated size: all mip levels, layers and samples, at least 4 KiB;
3-component formats count as 4 (Apple's GL pads them).
A resource that would take the total past the budget is refused like a failed
allocation (resource create fails, the guest's later use of it is refused);
freeing a resource gives its bytes back. OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_MB sets the budget,
0 turns it off; the default is a quarter of the Mac's memory. Screens and
cursors (resources with a SCANOUT, CURSOR or DISPLAY_TARGET bind) may go 256 MB
past it, so an app that fills the budget does not also stop the desktop's next
screen or cursor. QEMU's 2D resources (dumb buffers: console, plymouth, dumb
screens and cursors) get SCANOUT from qemu-virgl-2d-resource-scanout.patch;
without that patch they count as ordinary resources. The QEMU log says
the budget at start ("guest GPU memory budget: N MB") and logs refusals at the
1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th ... one. Blob pipe resources a guest made but never
claimed are now freed with their context including their GL storage (upstream
freed only the struct and kept the texture or buffer).
Why: nothing limited the host memory a guest can take with virgl resources
(QEMU's max_hostmem only covers 2D resources without virgl). A guest making
max-size textures in a loop could fill the Mac's memory (standards section 4:
no guest-controlled allocation without a limit).
Upstream: not sent; upstream has no host memory limit for vrend resources.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-resource-budget.c (build time, Apple's software
renderer): exact fit and refusal at the budget, mip chains, arrays, 3D, MSAA,
staging buffers, giving bytes back on unref and on context destroy (unclaimed
pipe resource), RGB32F charged as RGBA32F, the screen/cursor reserve, budget
off with 0.
